// TEST: Intrinsics Digamma Non-Positive (aborts)
// ABORT: error[BL8008]
// ABORT: digamma: argument must be positive
// ============================================================================
// digamma is defined for x > 0 only, on the same terms as lgamma (009) and
// sharing its diagnostic code: the reflection formula that would extend it to
// negative arguments is a second thing to keep bit-identical between
// blade_rt::digamma and its interpreter twin, and the callers this intrinsic
// exists for -- the gamma / beta / poisson log-likelihood GRADIENTS, which is
// what makes those models HMC-able -- never need it.
//
// A non-positive argument is therefore a caller bug and PANICS. Returning a
// silent NaN would be worse here than in lgamma's case: this value is a
// derivative, so it would seep into a gradient, and a NaN gradient surfaces as
// a sampler that quietly stops moving rather than as an error.
//
// The guard is `!(x > 0.0)`, so NaN is refused on the same branch. Both the
// compiled binary (blade_rt::panic) and the interpreter (InterpPanic) raise
// BL8008 with this message, which is what the differential gate compares.
// ============================================================================
let x = 0.0 - 1.0
let bad = digamma(x)
